	abstract = {Fruit flies or flies can not only discriminate visual motion direction through motion-sensitive neurons, but also continuously track the trajectories of moving targets in terms of the ring neuron’s rotational invariance characteristic as in the central complex. However, it still remains open how the neurons’ response properties can be simulated to integrate with intelligence optimization mechanisms, to probe into new-type and interdisciplinary multi-objective optimization approaches. Hereby, based on the fly visual response mechanism and the ring neurons’ distributed encoding characteristic, a genetic evolution-related multi-objective fly evolutionary neural network is achieved by sequentially connecting a new feedforward fly neural network to a genetic evolution-based update strategy. Specifically, the former outputs two types of real-time learning rates to regulate the transition amplitude and direction of each state, where a state represents a candidate solution in the decision space. The latter, which relies on the quality and sparsity of the current state matrix, aims to explore diverse potential states and drive them toward the Pareto front. Related to six recent and competitive multi-objective approaches and the microgrid integrated energy dispatch problem, the comparative experiments have clearly demonstrated that it is feasible to explore interdisciplinary multi-objective evolutionary neural networks, while the acquired visual evolutionary neural network can outperform the chosen approaches significantly. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
