	abstract = {As a core component of rotating machinery, bearing failures can lead to equipment downtime and even safety incidents. However, existing diagnostic methods still face bottlenecks of low accuracy and poor generalization capability when dealing with early stage weak faults and under varying operating conditions. This article proposes a quantum-inspired-classical hybrid deep learning model, featuring an improved quantum-inspired attention module (QIAM). This module simulates quantum superposition and entanglement through classical networks, thereby enhancing the discriminability of weak fault features. The model first employs a continuous wavelet transform (CWT) to convert vibration signals into time-frequency representations. Subsequently, a hybrid architecture combining a convolutional neural network (CNN) and QIAM is utilized for global feature learning. Experimental validation on bearing datasets from Case Western Reserve University (CWRU) and Paderborn University (PU) demonstrates that the proposed method achieves classification accuracies of 98.5% and 99.12%, respectively. It not only surpasses mainstream CNN and Transformer models but also exhibits higher parameter efficiency and superior global classification performance when compared with object detection models like YOLOv9-YOLOv11. This study provides a quantum-inspired technical pathway for fault diagnosis and offers an algorithmic foundation for future implementation on quantum hardware.  © 2026 IEEE.},
