	abstract = {The correlation between soil radon exhalation rate (Rn-ER) and its natural radionuclide content is complex, making quantification difficult using traditional regression methods. This hinders mapping the geogenic radon potential based on the available soil natural radionuclide data. To address these difficulties, this study explores the use of artificial neural networks (ANNs) and gene expression programming (GEP) models compared to the qualitative results of descriptive statistics for estimating the Rn-ER based on soil natural radionuclide content. The models were trained and tested using a worldwide dataset of soil radionuclide levels and Rn-ER. The results obtained from the ANNs model demonstrated its potential for accurately estimating the Rn-ER with higher accuracy and precision than the best-performing GEP model. The sensitivity analysis further confirmed the advantage of the ANNs in calculating the Rn-ER, considering the most significant variables affecting its value. The centered probability density function of the ratio of ANNs predicted Rn-ER to the measured values showed a normal distribution with a mean of 0.906 and a standard deviation of 0.192. © 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature.},
