	title = {T2S-EDNN: A Segmentation-Driven Specialized Ensemble Framework with Cluster-Adaptive Routing for Robust Chaotic Time Series Forecasting},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {IEEE Access},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3719509},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046849496?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Ton Duc Thang University, Center for Applied Information Technology, Ho Chi Minh City, 700000, Viet Nam; Ton Duc Thang University, Faculty of Information Technology, Ho Chi Minh City, 700000, Viet Nam; Ho Chi Minh City University of Foreign Languages and Information Technology, Department of IT, Ho Chi Minh City, 700000, Viet Nam; VSB—Technical University of Ostrava, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, Department of Computer Science, Ostrava, 70833, Czech Republic},
	abstract = {Chaotic time series, characterized by sensitive dependence on initial conditions, regime shifts, and high-order non-linear dependencies, remain a long-standing challenge for both classical statistical models and modern end-to-end deep learning predictors. Single global models, regardless of capacity, are forced to amortize their parameters across heterogeneous local dynamics, which dilutes specialization and harms generalization on unseen segments. We propose T2S-EDNN (Two-Stage Specialized Ensemble of Deep Neural Networks), a unified framework that decouples representation from prediction through three coordinated steps: (i) significant-extreme-point segmentation that extracts behavior-preserving sub-sequences with adaptive thresholds derived from local volatility; (ii) homothetic length normalization followed by hierarchical agglomerative clustering with an elbow-validated cluster count, producing a divide-and-conquer partition of local regimes; and (iii) per-cluster specialized ensembles of complementary deep architectures (Transformer, Autoencoder–LSTM, and Convolutional LSTM) that are routed at inference time via a nearest-prototype matcher. We provide a formal analysis of the bias–variance trade-off introduced by the cluster-conditioned ensemble and derive sufficient conditions under which the expected forecast error of T2S-EDNN improves upon that of a single global learner trained on the full mixture, namely when the regime-separability gap exceeds a closed-form threshold determined by the routing error. Extensive experiments on five benchmark chaotic series (Sunspot, Lorenz, New Delhi Weather, Mackey–Glass, and an Energy Consumption series) using a strict 70/10/20 train/validation/test split show that T2S-EDNN achieves the best overall results among fourteen baselines, including statistical (ARIMA, SARIMAX), classical machine learning (SVR), modern deep models (LSTM, TCN, TCN-CBAM, CNN-LSTM, Informer, DLinear, PatchTST, iTransformer), automated forecasting toolkits (AutoAI-TS, AutoGluon-TS), and our recently proposed LiteFormer (Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence, 2026). On Sunspot, the proposed model attains a 1-step RMSE of 0.0942 versus 0.1048 for LiteFormer, 0.1106 for iTransformer, and 0.1198 for TCN-CBAM, with relative improvements of 10.1%, 14.8%, and 21.4%, respectively. Across datasets and forecasting horizons (1- to 12-step), T2S-EDNN achieves the best average rank and the lowest overall error, but the margins over recent baselines such as DLinear, PatchTST, iTransformer, and LiteFormer remain deliberately moderate, reflecting the competitiveness of modern long-horizon forecasters. An open-source implementation of T2S-EDNN is available at https://ngocphien.info/t2s-ednn/. © 2013 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {AutoML; Chaotic time series; deep neural networks; ensemble learning; hierarchical clustering; Mixture of Experts; regime-aware forecasting; segmentation; time series forecasting; Transformer},
