	abstract = {Accurate sentiment analysis of financial news is essential for market trend prediction and investment risk management, yet existing approaches struggle to capture complex inter-entity dependencies inherent in financial discourse. This paper presents QESAD (Quantum-Enhanced Sentiment Analysis with Denoising), a novel framework integrating quantum-inspired neural networks, graph-based relational modeling, and adaptive denoising for financial sentiment classification. The framework comprises four components: Adaptive Semantic Denoising Network (ASDN), Maximum-Entropy Regularized Decision Transformer (ME-RDT), Quantum-Inspired Neural Networks (QINN) implementing amplitude-based encoding and entanglement transformations via classical simulation, and Quantum Graph Convolutional Network (QGCN) for entity relationship modeling.Evaluated on SEntFiN 1.0 (10,753 financial headlines, 2024–2025), QESAD achieves 99.8% accuracy, a statistically significant 2.6 percentage point improvement over FinBERT (97.2%), validated through paired t-test, bootstrap confidence intervals, and McNemar’s test (all p < .001). Ablation studies isolate quantum-inspired components’ contribution at 2.2 percentage points, while parameter-matched baselines prove that classical networks with 4 ×  capacity still fall 1.5 percentage points short, confirming gains stem from representational structure. Performance stratification reveals strategic advantages: modest 0.3 percentage point gains on single-entity headlines versus substantial 6.4 percentage point improvements on multi-entity conflicting sentiments (42% of test data). With 0.006 seconds inference latency, QESAD demonstrates practical viability for real-time financial analytics through classical simulation without quantum hardware. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
