	number = {13},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.3390/app15137521},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010210523?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {College of Engineering, University of Connecticut, Storrs, 06269, CT, United States},
	abstract = {Featured Application: The integrated-information measure Φ provides a practical tool for quantifying multipartite entanglement and identifying modular structures in many-body quantum states, thereby guiding tensor-network decompositions and variational ansätze. In quantum computing, Φ can suggest optimal qubit partitions to minimize information loss in error-correction procedures and inform distributed algorithm design. By constructing integration dendrograms, one can visualize hierarchical correlation patterns in quantum simulators and, speculatively, infer functional clusters in quantum-inspired models of neural networks. We introduce a quantum integrated-information measure (Formula presented.) for multipartite states within the Relational Quantum Dynamics (RQD) framework. (Formula presented.) is defined as the minimum quantum Jensen–Shannon distance between an n-partite density operator (Formula presented.) and any product state over a bipartition of its subsystems. We prove that its square root induces a genuine metric on state space and that (Formula presented.) is monotonic under all completely positive trace-preserving maps. Restricting the search to bipartitions yields a unique optimal split and a unique closest product state. From this geometric picture, we derive a canonical entanglement witness directly tied to (Formula presented.) and construct an integration dendrogram that reveals the full hierarchical correlation structure of (Formula presented.). We further show that there always exists an “optimal observer”—a channel or basis—that preserves (Formula presented.) better than any alternative. Finally, we propose a quantum Markov blanket theorem: the boundary of the optimal bipartition isolates subsystems most effectively. Our framework unites categorical enrichment, convex-geometric methods, and operational tools, forging a concrete bridge between integrated information theory and quantum information science. © 2025 by the author.},
