	abstract = {We propose a multi–microring-resonator deep-reservoir-computing (multi-MRR DRC) architecture in which add–drop microring resonators are interconnected via optical delay lines to form stacked reservoir layers. We validate the approach via numerical simulations on canonical time-series benchmarks. On the NARMA 10 task the three-MRR DRC achieves a normalized mean-square error (NMSE) of 0.071, corresponding to an 84.4% reduction relative to a single-ring RC baseline. On the Mackey–Glass prediction task the two-MRR DRC attains a 94.3% NMSE reduction. Analysis shows that multi-MRR layering synergistically enhances the reservoir's intrinsic nonlinearity and extends its effective memory, enabling richer temporal feature representations. These results indicate that the proposed architecture is a promising, compact photonic platform for low-latency temporal processing. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
