	doi = {10.1016/j.istruc.2025.109212},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105005107903?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Architecture, Chaoyang University of Technology, Taiwan},
	abstract = {According to the Housing Survey conducted by the Construction and Planning Agency in Taiwan, street houses accounted for 49.20 % of the housing types surveyed. This underscores the significance and ubiquity of street houses. However, they have experienced substantial damage and resulted in considerable loss of life and property in past major earthquakes. Therefore, developing a rapid and accurate method for assessing the seismic performance of existing street houses is a critical issue that warrants further investigation. In addition, when researchers apply artificial intelligence (AI) techniques to predict the seismic performance of street houses, they often encounter challenges in selecting the appropriate number of input factors and evaluating the strengths and limitations of the predictive models—issues that merit further discussion. This study employed collapsed ground acceleration as the indicator of seismic performance. However, calculating this parameter is time-consuming, costly, and requires field expertise, making it difficult for general engineers to carry out. To address these challenges and leverage expert knowledge, this study analyzed a dataset of 395 street houses and employed principal component analysis, data mining, and grey theory to investigate how to generalize seismic factors, cluster street houses, and determine the correlation between seismic factors and collapsed ground acceleration. The structural behavior of these buildings under seismic loading was modeled using artificial neural networks (ANNs) and gene expression programming (GEP), enabling the derivation of predictive equations tailored to this building type. A total of 8191 models were evaluated through sensitivity analysis, revealing that models with more than 10 input factors yielded superior results. The validated models provide a practical assessment tool for engineers and offer valuable insights into the seismic vulnerability of street houses. © 2025 Institution of Structural Engineers},
