	abstract = {Conventional time-delay reservoir computing (TDR) features compact topological structure and favorable hardware realizability, yet its integer-order chaotic driving units suffer limited dynamical richness, which restricts prediction performance improvement. Integrating the merits of discrete fractional-order chaotic systems in engineering deployment and complex dynamical evolution, this paper constructs a novel two-dimensional discrete fractional-order chaotic map and establishes a corresponding TDF-TDR framework with the proposed map as the driving module. This work deduces the complete mathematical formulation of the presented model and conducts systematic dynamical analysis; virtual nodes are generated via time-delay feedback of map states to replace traditional inter-node connections. Validated on four benchmark datasets including Mackey–Glass series, Melbourne temperature, UKERNA network traffic and sunspot observations, the proposed TDF-TDR achieves favorable overall prediction performance and exhibits moderate error accumulation over long-term prediction horizons. Ablation tests verify that fractional-order nonlinear excitation, two-dimensional state coupling and the nonlinear regulation factor constitute the core contributors to prediction accuracy. This research broadens the application scope of fractional-order chaos in reservoir computing and provides an effective solution for modeling complex nonlinear time series. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature B.V. 2026.},
