	abstract = {Human intelligence is increasingly understood as an emergent property of the cortexs cost efficient, multi-scale wiring; embedding analogous principles in artificial recurrent networks therefore promises brain-like economy and adaptability. Current physiological brain computer interfaces, however, still rely on anatomically agnostic reservoirs. We introduce the Hemispherically Hierarchical Lobe Reservoir(HH-LR), an Echo-State Network whose recurrent graph is neuro-anatomically constrained to reproduce hemispheric symmetry, lobe-wise heterogeneity and distance-dependent wiring economy. Neurons are partitioned into eight modules left/right ×{frontal,parietal,temporal,occipital} with Watts Strogatz micro-circuits inside each lobe, exponentially decaying intra-hemispheric shortcuts and sparse homotopic callosal bridges; the weight matrix is spectrally normalised to guarantee the Echo-State Property. A quadratic read-out captures cross-frequency interactions ubiquitous in bio-signals, while two capacity-allocation regimes are explored: uniform vs. MRI-derived volumetric ratios 4:3:2:1. Comprehensive experiments on BIDMC (PPG & respiration), MIT BIH Arrhythmia (ECG), Santa Fe B and the Sunspot Monthly series show that HH LR (i) cuts normalised RMSE by up to 77 %relative to the best non-anatomical reservoir, and (ii) derives complementary gains from its anatomical motifs: ablating homotopic callosal links or volumetric degree scaling erodes performance by 3 6 % each, confirming that both contribute additively to robustness. Secondary chaotic benchmarks (Lorenz-63, Rssler, Chen Ueta, Mackey-Glass) verify that HH LR retains the attractor-reconstruction accuracy of dense ESNs while operating with a fraction of the parameters. The proposed architecture thus bridges connectomics and reservoir computing, offering a principled, lightweight framework for real-time physiological signal interpretation. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
