	affiliations = {School of Traffic Engineering, Dalian Jiaotong University, Liaoning, 116028, China; School of Chemical and Environmental Engineering, Sichuan University of Science & Engneering, Sichuan, 643002, China; School of Materials Science and Engineering, Dalian Jiaotong University, Liaoning, 116028, China},
	abstract = {In order to enhance the safety of vehicles, maintain the strength of batteries and optimize the performances of batteries, it is essential to precisely predict the health state of Li ion batteries for electric vehicle (EV). However, how to accurately catch up with the early degradation signals of Li ion batteries is still a big difficulty. To overcome this difficulty, based on Transformer Model, a robust neural network is introduced in this paper. In contrast to previous methods, the transformer greatly enhanced predictive accuracy by skillfully fusing the temporary dependencies. This study introduces Quantum-Inspired channel attention (QICA)-Net, an innovative method that combines Quantum-Inspired Computing and conventional attention mechanisms. QICA-Net outperforms models like the Transformer and Efficient Channel Attention (ECA)-Net in key metrics. QICA-Net diminishes the Relative Percentage Error (RPE) to 0.0607. This represents an 86.8% improvement compared to the Transformer model, which exhibited an RPE of 0.4593. The ECA-Transformer model exhibited an RPE of 0.3836, however QICA-Net reduces it by 84.2%. The Mean Absolute Error (MAE) decreases to 0.0068 from 0.0760 in the Transformer model. This represents a 91.0% reduction. Compared to the ECA-Transformer model, which had an MAE of 0.0363, QICA-Net demonstrates a reduction of 81.3%. The Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) decreases from 0.0986 (Transformer) to 0.0090 (QICA-Net). This signifies a 90.9% reduction. These reductions render QICA-Net effective for computationally efficient inference. This study introduces a QICA mechanism. It utilizes linear algebra to simulate quantum rotation gates on GPU to capture complex non-linear features. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
