	abstract = {Hyperspectral image classification is the task of assigning a class label to each pixel in an image captured using hyperspectral sensors. The high dimensionality and rich spatial/spectral information present in the hyperspectral images often increase the feature selection and classification complexity. To overcome this issue, a Lightweight Convolutional Neural Network Ensemble Random forest and Gated Recurrent Unit (Light CERG Network) is proposed for the classification of hyperspectral images. Among the destination and source domains, the most stable compact and discriminative feature subsets are chosen through the use of the novel Adaptive Mutation Quantum-inspired Squirrel Search Algorithm (AM-QSSA). Dataset shift and feature relevance are the two different terms taken for the objective function. The Light CERG Network is used for hyperspectral image classification and it also incorporates both deep learning models and tree-based ensemble models. The Light CERG Network uses Random Forest(RF), Deep Learning(DL), Lightweight CNN, and Gated recurrent unit(GRU) to build a Stacking framework for hyperspectral image classification, using technical semantics and prior information as input variables. To avoid overfitting issues, the cross-validation approach has been utilized for creating the input, iteratively for a second-stage classifier. For proving the efficiency of the method more experiments are carried out using the average accuracy, overall accuracy, and kappa measures using these three popular UAV-HSI-Crop-Dataset, Pavia University HSI Dataset, and HSI—Datasets. © 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Bharati Vidyapeeth's Institute of Computer Applications and Management.},
