	abstract = {AbstractData re-uploading quantum circuits (DRQC) offer better expressiveness than feed-forward neural networks, but their practical use is constrained by the current development of quantum hardware. We propose a quantum-inspired data re-uploading network (Q-RUN), which approximately captures the Fourier-expressive advantages of DRQC without relying on any quantum resources. We conducted comprehensive evaluations of Q-RUN on seven representative tasks covering both data modeling and prediction. Compared with state-of-the-art neural layers, Q-RUN achieves simultaneous reductions in both test error and learnable parameters. For example, in molecular energy modeling, Q-RUN reduces modeling error by 91.16% while using 23.25% fewer parameters. Moreover, Q-RUN is designed as a modular layer that may serve as an alternative to fully connected layers, with potential performance benefits across different architectures. For instance, applying Q-RUN in large language model fine-tuning improves accuracy by 1.02%. These results demonstrate that principles from quantum machine learning can inspire the design of more expressive and parameter-efficient classical neural network layers. © 2026 Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
