	abstract = {Engineered cementitious composites with calcined clay limestone cement (LC3-ECC) are advanced construction material known for their environmental sustainability, low carbon footprint, and high tensile ductility. Owing to these advantages, LC3-ECC has gained increasing application in modern construction and infrastructure. However, its compressive strength (CS) is influenced by multiple interacting factors, such as the age (A), fiber Young's modulus (FY), cement-to-binder ratio (CBR), and superplasticizer content (S), etc., making accurate strength prediction a complex task. To address this issue, this study proposes a data-driven framework by developing two categories of predictive models: meta-heuristic (MH) optimized eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) models and gene expression programming (GEP) models. For the limited dataset containing 156 samples, an innovative k-means–based stratified partitioning strategy was introduced, together with a 5-fold cross-validation scheme to mitigate overfitting risks. Six different MH algorithms were further employed to optimize the hyperparameters of XGBoost. Experimental results demonstrate that the six MH-XGBoost models achieved exceptional predictive performance, with coefficients of determination (R<sup>2</sup>) exceeding 0.999 on the training set and 0.977 on the test set. Their predictive accuracy was significantly higher compared with traditional regression models, including random forest (RF), support vector regression (SVR), artificial neural network (ANN), and extreme learning machine (ELM), which were optimized using the SSA. Among them, the SSA-XGBoost model was identified as the best-performing approach, as reflected by its highest score of 0.0243 in the dynamic global performance indicator (D-GPI) evaluation and its statistically significant superiority confirmed through non-parametric tests. Further interpretation using SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) and Individual Conditional Expectation (ICE) provided insights into both the independent contributions and the interactive effects of features on CS. Based on these results, reference values for optimizing LC3-ECC mixtures were proposed. In addition, for GEP, principal component analysis (PCA) and SHAP were innovatively incorporated for dimensionality reduction, leading to the establishment of PCA-GEP and SHAP-GEP models. These models generate explicit mathematical expressions that enhance interpretability while maintaining reasonable predictive capability, with R<sup>2</sup> values exceeding 0.70 and 0.82, respectively. In summary, this study establishes a robust and interpretable predictive framework by combining MH-XGBoost and GEP techniques. The framework not only delivers superior predictive accuracy compared with conventional regression models but also provides valuable insights for the practical optimization of LC3-ECC formulations in engineering applications. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
